===2009===
The 2009 prize was judged by [[Jane Smiley]] (chair), [[Amit Chaudhuri]] and [[Andrey Kurkov]].<ref name="Munro">{{cite news|last=Flood|first=Alison|date=27 May 2009|title=Alice Munro wins Man Booker International prize|work=[[The Guardian]]|url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2009/may/27/alice-munro-man-booker-international-prize|access-date=22 May 2011|archive-date=8 March 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210308102110/https://www.theguardian.com/books/2009/may/27/alice-munro-man-booker-international-prize|url-status=live}}</ref> The nominees for the third Man Booker International Prize were announced on 18 March 2009 at [[The New York Public Library]].<ref>{{cite news|date=18 March 2009|title=E.L. Doctorow among Man Booker international book prize nominees|work=[[Cleveland.com]]|url=https://www.cleveland.com/books/2009/03/el_doctorow_among_man_booker_i.html|access-date=21 May 2024|archive-date=21 May 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521162743/https://www.cleveland.com/books/2009/03/el_doctorow_among_man_booker_i.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Canadian short story writer Alice Munro was named the winner of the prize in 2009 for her lifetime body of work.<ref name="Munro" /> Judge Jane Smiley said picking a winner had been "a challenge", but Munro had won the panel over.<ref name="Munro" /> On Munro's work, Smiley said "Her work is practically perfect. Any writer has to gawk when reading her because her work is very subtle and precise. Her thoughtfulness about every subject is so concentrated."<ref name="Munro" /> Munro, who said she was "totally amazed and delighted" at her win, received the award at [[Trinity College Dublin]] on 25 June.<ref name="Times" /><ref name="Munro" />
;Winner
