===2024===
The longlist was announced on 11 March 2024, the shortlist on 9 April 2024, and the winner on 21 May 2024, at a ceremony at [[Tate Modern]] in London, sponsored by [[Valentino (fashion house)|Maison Valentino]]. The judging panel was chaired by Canadian writer and broadcaster [[Eleanor Wachtel]], and consisted of Mojave American poet [[Natalie Diaz]], Sri Lankan British novelist [[Romesh Gunesekera]], South African artist [[William Kentridge]], and American writer, editor and translator Aaron Robertson. On choosing the six shortlisted books, Eleanor Wachtel said, "Our shortlist, while implicitly optimistic, engages with current realities of racism and oppression, global violence and ecological disaster."<ref>{{Cite web |title=The International Booker Prize 2024 |publisher=The Booker Prizes |url=https://thebookerprizes.com/the-booker-library/prize-years/international/2024 |access-date=22 February 2024 |archive-date=7 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240807002133/https://thebookerprizes.com/the-booker-library/prize-years/international/2024 |url-status=live }}</ref> The winner was [[Jenny Erpenbeck]] for her novel ''[[Kairos (novel)|Kairos]]'', translated from the German by [[Michael Hofmann]].<ref name="Gdn2024">{{cite news |last1=Creamer |first1=Ella |title=''Kairos'' by Jenny Erpenbeck wins International Booker prize |url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/article/2024/may/21/jenny-erpenbeck-michael-hofmann-win-2024-international-booker-prize-kairos |access-date=21 May 2024 |work=The Guardian |date=21 May 2024}}</ref> The judges' decision marked the first occasion the prize was won by either a German writer or a male translator.<ref name="Gdn2024"/>

