===2025===
The longlist was announced on 25 February 2025<ref name=Guardian20250225>{{cite news |last1=Creamer |first1=Ella |title=All 13 writers on International Booker longlist are first-time nominees |url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2025/feb/25/all-13-writers-on-international-booker-longlist-are-first-time-nominees |access-date=25 February 2025 |work=The Guardian |date=25 February 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Marshall |first1=Alex |title=Short Novels Dominate International Booker Prize Nominees |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/02/25/books/international-booker-prize-longlist.html |access-date=25 February 2025 |work=The New York Times |date=25 February 2025 |archive-date=25 February 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250225142724/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/02/25/books/international-booker-prize-longlist.html |url-status=live }}</ref> and the shortlist was published on 8 April 2025.<ref name="2025shortlist">{{cite web |last1=Creamer |first1=Ella |title='Mind-expanding books': International Booker prize shortlist announced |url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2025/apr/08/mind-expanding-books-international-booker-prize-shortlist-announced |website=The Guardian |access-date=8 April 2025 |date=8 April 2025}}</ref> The judging panel was chaired by English writer [[Max Porter (writer)|Max Porter]] and also consisted of Nigerian poet, director and photographer [[Caleb Femi]], writer and publishing director of ''[[Wasafiri]]'' Sana Goyal, South Korean writer and translator [[Anton Hur]], and English singer-songwriter [[Beth Orton]]. The winner, [[Banu Mushtaq]]'s ''[[Heart Lamp: Selected Stories]]'', was announced on 20 May 2025.<ref>{{cite web |title=The International Booker Prize 2025 |url=https://thebookerprizes.com/the-booker-library/prize-years/international/2025 |website=thebookerprizes.com |access-date=1 January 2025 |language=en |archive-date=1 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250101113122/https://thebookerprizes.com/the-booker-library/prize-years/international/2025 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Knight |first1=Lucy |title='Radical translation' of ''Heart Lamp'' by Banu Mushtaq wins International Booker prize |url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2025/may/20/radical-translation-of-heart-lamp-by-banu-mushtaq-wins-international-booker-prize |access-date=20 May 2025 |work=The Guardian |date=20 May 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Marshall |first1=Alex |title=In a First, a Story Collection Wins the International Booker Prize |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/05/20/books/review/international-booker-prize-winner.html |access-date=20 May 2025 |work=The New York Times |date=20 May 2025 |archive-date=23 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250723180103/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/05/20/books/review/international-booker-prize-winner.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

All 13 writers on the longlist were first-time nominees. The works selected for 2025 included several other firsts: the prize's first nominated translation from [[Kannada]] (''Heart Lamp''); the first nomination of a Romanian author ([[Mircea Cărtărescu|Cărtărescu]]); and the first nomination for an Iraqi translator ([[Sinan Antoon|Antoon]]). Translator [[Sophie Hughes]] appeared on the longlist for a record fifth time and on the shortlist for a record third time.<ref name=Guardian20250225/><ref name="2025shortlist"/> [[Deepa Bhasthi]] was the first Indian translator to win the award.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pylas |first1=Pan |title=Indian author Banu Mushtaq wins International Booker Prize with short story collection |url=https://apnews.com/article/international-booker-prize-india-mushtaq-bhasthi-02da7cdb8c0e7c4fa37040f59155bd9e |access-date=21 May 2025 |work=AP News |date=20 May 2025 |language=en |archive-date=21 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250521052103/https://apnews.com/article/international-booker-prize-india-mushtaq-bhasthi-02da7cdb8c0e7c4fa37040f59155bd9e |url-status=live }}</ref>
